/* Generated by scripts/build_docs.py. */
window.INFRATHON_DOCS={"tasks":{"attn-qat":{"task_id":"attn_qat_nvfp4_training_attention_h100_seed_v1","directory":"tasks/latest/attn-qat-nvfp4-training-attention-h100-v1-seed","launcher":"launch/attn-qat-train/run.sh","objective":"Accelerate the forward and backward attention operator used in video-model quantization-aware training while preserving the prescribed NVFP4 fake-quantization behavior.","workspace":["Seed: kernel_v001 provides a complete Triton implementation.","Edit candidates/kernel_vNNN/submission.py, keeping the forward and backward interfaces."],"requirements":["Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","PyTorch 2.8 / CUDA 12.9 / Triton task environment.","BF16 attention tensors with Wan2.1-T2V-1.3B, LTX-2, and Kandinsky5 geometries."],"evaluation":"Measures effective training throughput across three model families after FP32-reference output and gradient checks, edge fixtures, input preservation, and intermediate-storage checks pass.","result_intro":"This task accelerates the forward and backward attention operator used to fine-tune video diffusion models with NVFP4 quantization-aware training.","metric_summary":"The score is effective TFLOPS for a checkpointed training step: fixed work divided by twice the forward time plus backward time. Seven-trial median throughputs are combined geometrically, giving Wan2.1-T2V-1.3B, LTX-2 and Kandinsky5 equal weight.","setting":"The starting candidate is a correct Triton port of FastVideo’s SM90 attention implementation. Four workload cells represent two Wan2.1-T2V-1.3B configurations, LTX-2 and Kandinsky5, with head dimensions of 64 or 128 and different sequence lengths. Inputs remain BF16 while queries, keys, values and attention probabilities undergo the specified NVFP4 quantize–dequantize operations. Evaluation covers this attention layer rather than an entire video-model training run.","launch_setup":"nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,uuid --format=csv,noheader","launch_command":"WORK_GPU=0 JUDGE_GPU=1 launch/attn-qat-train/run.sh \\\n  --legacy-dual-gpu \\\n  --model MODEL_ID --effort max --hours 12 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","reader_overview":"Video models need attention calculations in both the forward pass and the gradient calculation during training. The agent speeds up this attention routine.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Model and data","description":"Attention workloads from Wan2.1-T2V-1.3B, LTX-2 and Kandinsky5, using the prescribed four-bit quantization simulation."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"Four test configurations: two for Wan and one each for LTX-2 and Kandinsky5. Timing combines two forward calls and one backward call."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A working attention implementation written in Triton."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"How attention work is divided across the GPU, how intermediate values are stored or recomputed, and how rounding and gradient calculations are combined."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures attention training speed, including the required quantization and gradient computation.","rule":"Score = attention training work ÷ (2 × forward time + backward time), expressed in TFLOPS and combined across cases."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB)."},"wgrad":{"task_id":"standalone_k_grouped_fp8_wgrad_h100_scratch_v2","directory":"tasks/latest/standalone-k-grouped-fp8-wgrad-h100-v2-scratch","launcher":"launch/standalone-kgrouped-wgrad/run.sh","objective":"Implement fast grouped FP8 weight-gradient matrix multiplication, accumulating each expert group into its existing FP32 gradient tensor.","workspace":["Scratch: candidates/kernel_v001/submission.py starts empty.","Implement the grouped weight-gradient entry point in versioned Triton candidates; use FINAL_VERSION to select the submission."],"requirements":["Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","PyTorch 2.8 / CUDA 12.9 / Triton task environment.","FP8 matrices, block scales, group lengths, and FP32 gradient buffers."],"evaluation":"Measures effective TFLOPS across coarse and fine grouping workloads. Numerical references, complete writes, unchanged inputs, and memory-safety checks establish correctness.","result_intro":"Implement the weight-gradient matrix multiplication used by mixture-of-experts training, starting from an empty Triton candidate.","metric_summary":"The score is effective TFLOPS using the actual sum of group reduction lengths and the median of five throughput trials per case. Coarse and fine grouping families receive equal weight through geometric means.","setting":"The workload follows DeepGEMM’s K-grouped training GEMM, but the Work image supplies no GEMM implementation. Seven scored configurations span coarse and fine expert groupings, with fresh group lengths in each trial. The supported domain also includes 1–128 groups, arbitrary output dimensions from 1 to 8192, and empty groups. An aligned fast path must therefore coexist with correct handling of irregular dimensions and reductions.","launch_setup":"nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,uuid --format=csv,noheader","launch_command":"WORK_GPU=0 JUDGE_GPU=1 launch/standalone-kgrouped-wgrad/run.sh \\\n  --legacy-dual-gpu \\\n  --model MODEL_ID --effort max --hours 12 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","reader_overview":"Training a model with multiple experts requires calculating gradients that tell each expert how to adjust its weights. The agent builds a fast GPU routine for these grouped calculations, using eight-bit inputs.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Workload","description":"Grouped matrix multiplications for expert weight gradients, using eight-bit inputs and existing gradients stored at higher precision."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"Seven performance configurations cover coarse and fine expert groupings. Group sizes change between trials."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"An empty implementation with a defined interface, public tests and the required calculation described in the task."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"The complete GPU implementation: dividing matrix work into blocks, distributing groups, combining partial results and handling small or uneven groups."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures how quickly the kernel computes the required expert weight gradients. Throughput uses the actual group sizes; the coarse- and fine-group workloads have equal weight.","rule":"Score = useful matrix operations ÷ execution time, expressed in TFLOPS and combined across cases."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB)."},"flux-moe":{"task_id":"flux_moe_ag_scatter_grouped_gemm_dual_h100_seed_v1","directory":"tasks/latest/flux-moe-ag-scatter-grouped-gemm-dual-h100-v1-seed","launcher":"launch/flux-moe-ag/run.sh","objective":"Accelerate a two-GPU MoE input-side primitive that gathers tokens, scatters them by expert routing, and performs grouped matrix multiplication.","workspace":["Seed: kernel_v001 is a self-contained port of the Flux Triton layer-0 path.","Edit the candidate submission.py to change communication/computation scheduling, tiling, staging, and synchronization."],"requirements":["Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","PyTorch 2.8 / CUDA 12.9 runtime with bidirectional GPU peer access.","Host CUDA PyTorch with NCCL and symmetric-memory support for the GPU-pair preflight."],"evaluation":"Measures useful per-rank throughput using the slower rank’s time. Output accuracy, complete writes, fresh-input execution, input preservation, and rank agreement must pass.","result_intro":"Optimize the input-side primitive of a tensor-parallel mixture-of-experts layer on two GPUs.","metric_summary":"Effective TFLOPS divides useful matrix work per rank by the slower rank’s time, including all-gather, scatter, GEMM and synchronization. Seven trials determine each case’s median time; geometric means give the heavy- and moderate-communication families equal weight.","setting":"The seed is a self-contained Triton adaptation of Flux’s layer-0 path, using remote-pull producers and grouped-GEMM consumers on a peer-connected GPU pair. Five scored cells represent Qwen3-Next, Qwen3-MoE, gpt-oss, Mixtral and DeepSeek configurations. Expert counts, selected experts and per-rank channel widths vary across these cells. Routing decisions are supplied as inputs; applying routing weights or activation functions and combining expert outputs are outside this primitive.","launch_setup":"# Select the NVLink pair allocated to this run.\nnvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,uuid --format=csv,noheader","launch_command":"launch/flux-moe-ag/run.sh \\\n  --model MODEL_ID --effort max --hours 12 \\\n  --gpus 0,1 --no-wait --auth /path/to/auth.json","reader_overview":"A mixture-of-experts model sends each token to a small selection of expert networks. This task speeds up gathering tokens across two GPUs, grouping them by expert, and multiplying them by the expert weights.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Model and data","description":"Five expert-model configurations representing Qwen3-Next, Qwen3-MoE, gpt-oss, Mixtral and DeepSeek."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"Each configuration supplies token data, expert weights and fixed routing choices. Both GPUs exchange token data and compute their assigned expert outputs."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A working Triton implementation adapted from Flux, with token gathering, expert grouping and matrix multiplication already connected."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"The order of transfers and computation, how GPU workers share those jobs, and how data is staged for each expert."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures how quickly the two GPUs complete token collection, expert routing, and matrix multiplication together. Each measurement uses the slower GPU’s time, and the two communication-load families have equal weight.","rule":"Score = useful matrix operations per GPU ÷ complete execution time, expressed in TFLOPS and combined across cases."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB).","launch_note":"This launcher uses the same GPU pair for Worker and Judge."},"orpo":{"task_id":"qwen3_1p7b_orpo_training_optimizer_dual_h100_v4","directory":"tasks/latest/qwen3-1p7b-orpo-training-optimizer-dual-h100-v4-cross-matrix-seed","launcher":"launch/qwen3-orpo/run.sh","objective":"Accelerate complete two-GPU ORPO training steps for Qwen3-1.7B while retaining the behavior of the official TRL objective and full-parameter optimizer updates.","workspace":["Seed: solutions/objective_v001/ selects the installed official TRL objective.","Each version contains candidate.py and __init__.py; implement build_objective(contract), with an optional declarative build_model_patch(contract).","Select the submitted version through FINAL_VERSION."],"requirements":["Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","Qwen3-1.7B, TL;DR preference data, and the provided TRL 0.12.1 / FSDP runtime."],"evaluation":"Compares complete-step throughput with an official TRL reference measured in the same evaluation. Losses, gradients, optimizer state, nonzero parameter updates, and memory use must meet the reference checks.","result_intro":"Accelerate complete Qwen3-1.7B preference-training steps while preserving the official TRL ORPO objective and full-parameter updates.","metric_summary":"The score is 100 times geometric-mean training speedup across five workloads, using each workload’s median of four candidate/reference pairs. Throughput counts completion-target tokens over complete optimizer-step time, including communication and updates.","setting":"Qwen3-1.7B trains in BF16 on TL;DR chosen/rejected preferences using two GPUs and FSDP1 FULL_SHARD. Its tied embedding and output head has a 151,936-token vocabulary, making projection and materialized logits a substantial cost. Five workloads vary stored sequence length from 512 to 2048 and per-rank pair batch from 2 to 8. The provided seed invokes TRL 0.12.1; this is full-parameter AdamW training, not LoRA.","launch_setup":"# Select an allocated NVLink pair and copy its UUIDs.\nnvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,uuid --format=csv,noheader\nexport ORPO_GPU_INDICES=0,1\nexport ORPO_GPU_UUIDS=GPU-...,GPU-...","launch_command":"launch/qwen3-orpo/run.sh \\\n  --model MODEL_ID --effort max --hours 24 \\\n  --no-wait --auth /path/to/auth.json","reader_overview":"Qwen3-1.7B is trained on preferred and rejected summaries so that it learns to favor the preferred response. The agent accelerates the training computation.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Model and data","description":"Qwen3-1.7B and TL;DR summary preference pairs, using the TRL training library as the reference."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"Five workloads vary sequence length and batch size. Each measurement runs five warm-up training steps followed by ten timed steps."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A working baseline that calls the official preference-training objective."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"The calculation of word scores and training loss, plus supported implementations of model layers. The supplied runner controls the data, optimizer and training schedule."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Compares complete ORPO training-step throughput with the reference implementation in the TRL training library on the same hardware. A score of 100 matches the reference.","rule":"Score = 100 × geometric mean of training speedup across five workloads."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB).","launch_note":"This launcher uses the same GPU pair for Worker and Judge."},"gkd":{"task_id":"qwen3_4b_to_1p7b_gkd_distillation_optimizer_dual_h100_v1","directory":"tasks/latest/qwen3-4b-to-1p7b-gkd-distillation-optimizer-dual-h100-v1-official-trl-seed","launcher":"launch/qwen3-gkd/run.sh","objective":"Accelerate generalized knowledge-distillation training from a frozen Qwen3-4B teacher into Qwen3-1.7B while preserving the full training computation.","workspace":["Seed: solutions/objective_v001/candidate.py provides the official TRL generalized-JSD objective.","Implement build_objective(contract); optional build_model_patch and build_teacher_patch interfaces expose model-computation optimizations.","Submit versioned solutions with FINAL_VERSION and notes.md."],"requirements":["Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB.","Frozen Qwen3-4B teacher, trainable Qwen3-1.7B student, and TL;DR prompt/completion data.","Provided PyTorch 2.8 / CUDA 12.9 / FSDP runtime with the official TRL objective."],"evaluation":"Measures training-throughput speedup against the paired official TRL reference. Objective tests, student updates, frozen-teacher preservation, and memory checks must pass.","result_intro":"Accelerate off-policy knowledge-distillation steps from a frozen Qwen3-4B teacher into a trainable Qwen3-1.7B student.","metric_summary":"The score is 100 times geometric-mean speedup across five training workloads, taking the median of four paired candidate/reference ratios per workload. Timing includes teacher and student forwards, loss, backward, communication and optimizer updates.","setting":"The frozen BF16 teacher is replicated on each of two GPUs; the BF16 student uses FSDP FULL_SHARD and full-parameter AdamW. Both read TL;DR prompts with fixed chosen completions, so the timed workload does not generate new student trajectories. The objective matches TRL 0.12.1’s generalized JSD, with a 151,936-token vocabulary. Five workload rows vary sequence length and batch size, while prompt and padding positions are excluded from the completion-target count.","launch_setup":"# Select the NVLink pair allocated to this run.\nnvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,uuid --format=csv,noheader","launch_command":"launch/qwen3-gkd/run.sh \\\n  --model MODEL_ID --effort max --hours 24 \\\n  --gpus 0,1 --no-wait --auth /path/to/auth.json","reader_overview":"A smaller Qwen3-1.7B student learns from the predictions of a frozen Qwen3-4B teacher on supplied summaries. The agent accelerates the complete training step, including the teacher’s computation.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Model and data","description":"A Qwen3-4B teacher, Qwen3-1.7B student and TL;DR prompts with fixed summary completions."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"Five workloads vary sequence length and batch size. Each measurement runs five warm-up steps and ten timed steps, including teacher prediction and student training."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A working baseline using the reference distillation loss from the TRL training library."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"How teacher and student word scores and the training loss are computed, plus supported model-layer implementations. The teacher stays frozen and the student receives full model updates."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Compares complete teacher–student training-step throughput with the reference implementation in the TRL training library. A score of 100 matches the reference.","rule":"Score = 100 × geometric mean of training speedup across five workloads."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair. H100 NVL (94 GB).","launch_note":"This launcher uses the same GPU pair for Worker and Judge."},"opcd":{"task_id":"miles_qwen3_opcd_2xh100_v3","directory":"tasks/latest/miles-qwen3-opcd-2xh100-v3-seed","launcher":null,"objective":"Implement context distillation in Miles so a Qwen3-1.7B student learns from a frozen same-size teacher given a medical system prompt, then improve the complete training pipeline.","workspace":["Seed: Miles framework with the context-conditioned teacher and analytic top-k loss to implement.","Edit repo/, task_entry.py, config/, and notes.md.","task_entry.py exposes run for real training and probe for updates on supplied trajectories."],"requirements":["Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","Qwen3-1.7B student and frozen teacher, MedMCQA data, and the teacher’s medical system prompt.","Provided Miles runtime; 24-CPU quota and 256 GiB host RAM per container.","Set `MILES_SCRATCH_ROOT` on a data disk with at least 200 GiB free; the system root filesystem also needs 20 GiB free.","Export `SFORGE_CPUSET=auto` to use the CPU pinning policy of the released qualification."],"evaluation":"Measures held-out medical accuracy gained per audited training hour. The quality threshold and checks linking samples, training updates, published weights, and evaluated weights must pass.","result_intro":"Implement context distillation in Miles so a Qwen3-1.7B student learns medical answering behavior from its own initial weights acting as a frozen teacher with a medical system prompt.","metric_summary":"The score is held-out MedMCQA accuracy gained in percentage points per audited training hour, averaged over three evaluations on 2,436 questions. At least 6.5 points of improvement is required; timing includes generation, learning and final weight publication.","setting":"The supplied Miles checkout combines SGLang generation, Megatron-Core training and Ray orchestration, but its context-distillation loss is unfinished. The fixed workload contains 32 rounds of 512 MedMCQA prompts with one response per prompt and a 512-token response limit. Teacher and student start from the same Qwen3-1.7B snapshot. Each round uses a training-side student top-256 distribution, performs one AdamW update and publishes new student weights.","scratch_setup":"# Choose a writable directory on your non-system data disk.\nexport MILES_SCRATCH_ROOT=/absolute/path/on-data-disk/infrathon-scratch\nmkdir -p \"$MILES_SCRATCH_ROOT\"","launch_setup":"export SFORGE_CPUSET=auto","launch_command":"launch/miles-opcd/run.sh --config launch/miles-opcd/run.env --dry-run\nlaunch/miles-opcd/run.sh --config launch/miles-opcd/run.env --foreground","config_setup":"cp launch/miles-opcd/codex.env.example launch/miles-opcd/run.env\n# Use the paper’s 24-hour budget.\nsed -i 's/^TASK_TIMEOUT=.*/TASK_TIMEOUT=86400/' launch/miles-opcd/run.env","reader_overview":"A Qwen3-1.7B student must learn medical knowledge from a frozen teacher that receives an additional medical instruction. The agent implements this learning process in the Miles training framework, then makes the full cycle of answer generation, teacher feedback and student updates faster.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Model and data","description":"Teacher and student begin from the same Qwen3-1.7B model. Training uses MedMCQA questions; only the teacher receives the additional medical instruction."},{"name":"Training setup","description":"32 rounds of 512 questions, with one student answer per question and a limit of 512 generated tokens. Every round updates the student and sends its new weights to the answer generator."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A Miles training setup with the context-distillation loss unfinished."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"Teacher feedback, the prescribed learning loss, and the scheduling of generation, training and weight transfer within Miles."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Rewards medical-answer accuracy gained per hour of training, measured on 2,436 held-out MedMCQA questions. The trained student must improve by at least 6.5 percentage points to receive a positive score.","rule":"Score = accuracy gain in percentage points ÷ training hours."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB).","launch_note":"This launcher uses the same GPU pair for Worker and Judge."},"opd":{"task_id":"miles_qwen3_opd_scratch_2xh100_v2","directory":"tasks/latest/miles-qwen3-opd-scratch-2xh100-v2-seed","launcher":null,"objective":"Build the online distillation loop in Miles: Qwen3-1.7B generates math solutions, a frozen Qwen3-4B teacher scores their tokens, and the student learns and publishes updated weights.","workspace":["Seed: supplied Miles framework with the OPD loop to implement.","Edit repo/, task_entry.py, config/, and notes.md.","Implement the run and probe entry points in task_entry.py."],"requirements":["Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","Qwen3-1.7B student, frozen Qwen3-4B teacher, and DAPO-Math data.","Provided Miles runtime; 24 CPUs and 256 GiB host RAM per container.","Set `MILES_SCRATCH_ROOT` on a data disk with at least 200 GiB free; the system root filesystem also needs 20 GiB free.","Export `SFORGE_CPUSET=auto` to use the CPU pinning policy of the released qualification."],"evaluation":"Measures held-out math-accuracy gain per audited training hour. Teacher/student probabilities, loss and gradients, sample usage, weight publication, and the quality threshold are checked.","result_intro":"Build sampled-token on-policy distillation inside Miles: Qwen3-1.7B generates mathematical solutions, a frozen Qwen3-4B teacher scores the sampled tokens, and the student learns from their probability differences.","metric_summary":"The score is math-accuracy improvement in percentage points per audited training hour, using three evaluations on 2,000 held-out DAPO-Math problems. A gain of at least 1.6 points is required; timing includes rollout, teacher scoring, learning and final weight publication.","setting":"The Miles starter can launch training and export weights, but the on-policy distillation feedback and step records are missing. A frozen Qwen3-4B teacher guides a Qwen3-1.7B student for 32 rounds of 128 DAPO-Math prompts. Each prompt produces one response of at most 4,096 tokens with thinking disabled. Student and teacher sampled-token log-probabilities define the fixed advantage, followed by a PPO-clipped update and publication each round.","scratch_setup":"# Choose a writable directory on your non-system data disk.\nexport MILES_SCRATCH_ROOT=/absolute/path/on-data-disk/infrathon-scratch\nmkdir -p \"$MILES_SCRATCH_ROOT\"","launch_setup":"export SFORGE_CPUSET=auto","launch_command":"launch/miles-opd/run.sh --config launch/miles-opd/run.env --dry-run\nlaunch/miles-opd/run.sh --config launch/miles-opd/run.env --foreground","config_setup":"cp launch/miles-opd/codex.env.example launch/miles-opd/run.env\n# Use the paper’s 24-hour budget.\nsed -i 's/^TASK_TIMEOUT=.*/TASK_TIMEOUT=86400/' launch/miles-opd/run.env","reader_overview":"A Qwen3-1.7B student generates math solutions and learns from a frozen Qwen3-4B teacher’s assessment of those same responses. The agent builds this online learning loop in the Miles training framework and speeds up generation, feedback and training.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Model and data","description":"A Qwen3-1.7B student, frozen Qwen3-4B teacher and DAPO-Math problems."},{"name":"Training setup","description":"32 rounds of 128 problems, with one response per problem and a limit of 4,096 generated tokens. Each round trains on its responses and sends the updated student weights to the generator."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A starter that launches Miles training and exports model weights, but has no teacher feedback or required training-step records."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"Teacher scoring, the prescribed student learning calculation, and how generation, training and weight transfer run together in Miles."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Rewards math-answer accuracy gained per hour of training, measured on 2,000 held-out DAPO-Math problems. The trained student must improve by at least 1.6 percentage points to receive a positive score.","rule":"Score = accuracy gain in percentage points ÷ training hours."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB).","launch_note":"This launcher uses the same GPU pair for Worker and Judge."},"alfworld":{"task_id":"miles_qwen3_alfworld_agentic_rl_2xh100_v1","directory":"tasks/latest/miles-qwen3-alfworld-agentic-rl-2xh100-v1-seed","launcher":null,"objective":"Connect ALFWorld to Miles and train Qwen3-4B from multi-turn game interactions with GRPO, then accelerate the complete rollout, learning, and weight-publication loop.","workspace":["Seed: Miles and environment dependencies with ALFWorld integration and run/probe entry points to implement.","Edit repo/, task_entry.py, config/, and notes.md.","Use the same model and learning implementation for real environment runs and recorded-trajectory probes."],"requirements":["Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","Qwen3-4B, ALFWorld 0.4.2, TextWorld 1.7.0, and json_2.1.1 game data.","Provided Miles runtime; 24-CPU quota and 256 GiB host RAM per container.","Set `MILES_SCRATCH_ROOT` on a data disk with at least 300 GiB free; the system root filesystem also needs 20 GiB free.","Export `SFORGE_CPUSET=auto` to use the CPU pinning policy of the released qualification."],"evaluation":"Measures held-out game-success gain per audited training hour. Environment replay, learning computation, sample-to-update consistency, final weights, and a success-improvement threshold are checked.","result_intro":"Integrate ALFWorld’s text household games into Miles and train Qwen3-4B through multi-turn GRPO.","metric_summary":"The score is held-out game-success improvement in percentage points per audited training hour, measured over three greedy evaluations on 1,134 games. At least 15 points of improvement is required; the duration includes environment interaction and learning through final weight publication.","setting":"Miles supplies the inference, training and checkpoint infrastructure, while the ALFWorld integration and run/probe entry points must be implemented. Training comprises 12 rounds of 16 games, with eight episodes per game for group-relative learning. Each episode allows at most 30 actions and 256 generated tokens per turn. Qwen3-4B receives the observation and admissible actions, preserves its full token history, and earns reward 1 only for winning.","scratch_setup":"# Choose a writable directory on your non-system data disk.\nexport MILES_SCRATCH_ROOT=/absolute/path/on-data-disk/infrathon-scratch\nmkdir -p \"$MILES_SCRATCH_ROOT\"","launch_setup":"export SFORGE_CPUSET=auto","launch_command":"launch/miles-alfworld/run.sh --config launch/miles-alfworld/run.env --dry-run\nlaunch/miles-alfworld/run.sh --config launch/miles-alfworld/run.env --foreground","config_setup":"cp launch/miles-alfworld/codex.env.example launch/miles-alfworld/run.env\n# Use the paper’s 24-hour budget.\nsed -i 's/^TASK_TIMEOUT=.*/TASK_TIMEOUT=86400/' launch/miles-alfworld/run.env","reader_overview":"Qwen3-4B learns to complete household tasks by interacting with the ALFWorld text environment and receiving a reward when it succeeds. The agent connects these interactions to the Miles training framework, then accelerates game collection, model updates and weight transfer.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Model and data","description":"Qwen3-4B and ALFWorld games. The model reads observations and available actions, then chooses actions through generated text."},{"name":"Training setup","description":"12 rounds of 16 games, with eight attempts per game. Each attempt allows up to 30 actions and 256 generated tokens per turn; only winning earns a reward."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"Miles supplies model generation, training and checkpoint support; the game integration and task entry points must be implemented."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"The connection to the game environment, preparation of game interactions for learning, and scheduling of generation, model updates and weight transfer."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Rewards improvement in the fraction of household games completed successfully per hour of training. Evaluation uses 1,134 held-out games, and success must improve by at least 15 percentage points.","rule":"Score = game-success gain in percentage points ÷ training hours."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB).","launch_note":"This launcher uses the same GPU pair for Worker and Judge."},"sd35":{"task_id":"miles_diffusion_sd35m_mopd_2xh100_v2","directory":"tasks/latest/miles-diffusion-sd35m-mopd-2xh100-v2-seed","launcher":null,"objective":"Add multi-teacher online distillation to Miles Diffusion so one SD3.5 Medium LoRA learns from OCR and aesthetics teachers, then accelerate training and publication.","workspace":["Seed: framework source and launch parameters with teacher paths, distillation loss, and training records to implement.","Edit repo/, task_entry.py, config/, and notes.md.","task_entry.py run executes the specified routed training workload and publishes the student LoRA."],"requirements":["Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","Stable Diffusion 3.5 Medium, OCR and aesthetics teachers, and routed prompt data.","Provided Miles Diffusion runtime with PaddleOCR; 256 GiB host RAM per container.","Set `MILES_SCRATCH_ROOT` on a data disk with at least 500 GiB free; the system root filesystem also needs 20 GiB free.","Export `SFORGE_CPUSET=auto` to use the CPU pinning policy of the released qualification."],"evaluation":"Combines OCR/PickScore teacher-gap closure with replay throughput. Both route-specific quality and KL gates must pass, together with checks of the real training run, optimizer steps, and published LoRA.","result_intro":"Implement multi-teacher on-policy distillation for Stable Diffusion 3.5 Medium in miles_diffusion.","metric_summary":"The score averages the fraction of the OCR and PickScore teacher gaps closed, then divides by median replay hours across three segments. Quality comes from the real training run’s final LoRA; both routes must close at least 25% of their gap and pass their KL-improvement gates.","setting":"The starter has FSDP2 training, Ray and sglang-diffusion rollout infrastructure but no teacher path. Stable Diffusion 3.5 Medium trains a rank-32, alpha-64 LoRA over 64 rounds of eight routed prompt rows, with eight samples per row. Sampling uses 512×512 images and a ten-step CPS trajectory with a three-step stochastic window. Each round performs two AdamW updates and publishes the student LoRA before the next round begins.","after_pull_command":".venv/bin/python scripts/infrathon/assets.py own \"$TASK\" \\\n  --image miles_diffusion_opd.judge.miles_diffusion_sd35m_mopd_2xh100_v2:local","scratch_setup":"# Choose a writable directory on your non-system data disk.\nexport MILES_SCRATCH_ROOT=/absolute/path/on-data-disk/infrathon-scratch\nmkdir -p \"$MILES_SCRATCH_ROOT\"","launch_setup":"export SFORGE_CPUSET=auto","launch_command":"launch/miles-diffusion-opd/run.sh --config launch/miles-diffusion-opd/run.env --dry-run\nlaunch/miles-diffusion-opd/run.sh --config launch/miles-diffusion-opd/run.env --foreground","config_setup":"cp launch/miles-diffusion-opd/codex.env.example launch/miles-diffusion-opd/run.env\n# Use the paper’s 24-hour budget.\nsed -i 's/^TASK_TIMEOUT=.*/TASK_TIMEOUT=86400/' launch/miles-diffusion-opd/run.env","reader_overview":"A Stable Diffusion 3.5 Medium image generator learns from two teachers, one specializing in readable text and the other in visual aesthetics. The agent implements their guidance in the Miles Diffusion training framework and accelerates training of a small student adapter.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Model and data","description":"Stable Diffusion 3.5 Medium, two frozen teacher adapters and prompts assigned to the appropriate teacher. One small trainable adapter combines what the student learns."},{"name":"Training setup","description":"64 rounds of eight prompts, with eight images per prompt. Images are 512 × 512 pixels and use ten sampling steps; each round makes two training updates and sends the updated adapter to the generators."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A working Miles Diffusion framework with image generation and training infrastructure, but no teacher-guided training path."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"How the selected teacher guides each generated image, how training work is batched and overlapped, and how the updated adapter reaches the image generators."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures how much of the gap to the two teachers the student closes per hour of replayed training. Text-reading accuracy and aesthetics contribute equally. Both must close at least 25% of their teacher gap and pass the loss checks.","rule":"Score = average fraction of the teacher quality gap closed ÷ replay hours."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB).","launch_note":"This launcher uses the same GPU pair for Worker and Judge."},"wan22":{"task_id":"miles_diffusion_wan22_dancegrpo_2xh100_v2","directory":"tasks/latest/miles-diffusion-wan22-dancegrpo-2xh100-v2-seed","launcher":null,"objective":"Implement DanceGRPO video training for Wan2.2, including its sampling transitions, grouped noise, HPSv3 rewards, learning updates, and complete weight publication.","workspace":["Seed: video-model/framework support with reward, real-training, and replay-service entry points to implement.","Edit repo/task_entry.py and the Miles Diffusion source in repo/; submit repo/ with notes.md.","Permitted engine changes use the documented overlay files."],"requirements":["Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","Wan2.2-TI2V-5B, HPSv3, and task-provided prompts, videos, and diffusion trajectories.","Provided Miles Diffusion runtime; 256 GiB host RAM and 128 GiB shared memory per container.","Set `MILES_SCRATCH_ROOT` on a data disk with at least 300 GiB free; the system root filesystem also needs 20 GiB free.","Export `SFORGE_CPUSET=auto` to use the CPU pinning policy of the released qualification."],"evaluation":"Measures fixed-workload replay throughput after checking frame rewards, sampling, training updates, and publication. A separate evaluation of the real run’s final model must pass the HPSv3 quality gate.","result_intro":"Implement DanceGRPO video training for Wan2.2-TI2V-5B in miles_diffusion, including stochastic sampling transitions, grouped noise and official HPSv3 frame rewards.","metric_summary":"The score is 3,600 divided by the median seconds for three fixed replays, each containing 16 prompts with eight videos per prompt. It measures replays per hour; the real run’s final-model HPSv3 reward must be at least the baseline minus 0.28.","setting":"Wan2.2-TI2V-5B runs in text-to-video mode with full transformer training; the text encoder and VAE stay frozen. The fixed recipe generates nine-frame 704×1280 videos using ten steps, CFG 5 and Dance-SDE noise. Groups of eight share initial noise but use independent stochastic-transition noise. A real run performs three rounds of eight prompts, with two optimizer steps and a complete transformer publication per round; separate replays measure the training-side throughput.","after_pull_command":".venv/bin/python scripts/infrathon/assets.py own \"$TASK\" \\\n  --image miles_diffusion_wan.judge.miles_diffusion_wan22_dancegrpo_2xh100_v2:local","scratch_setup":"# Choose a writable directory on your non-system data disk.\nexport MILES_SCRATCH_ROOT=/absolute/path/on-data-disk/infrathon-scratch\nmkdir -p \"$MILES_SCRATCH_ROOT\"","launch_setup":"export SFORGE_CPUSET=auto","launch_command":"launch/miles-diffusion-wan/run.sh --config launch/miles-diffusion-wan/run.env --dry-run\nlaunch/miles-diffusion-wan/run.sh --config launch/miles-diffusion-wan/run.env --foreground","config_setup":"cp launch/miles-diffusion-wan/codex.env.example launch/miles-diffusion-wan/run.env\n# Use the paper’s 24-hour budget.\nsed -i 's/^TASK_TIMEOUT=.*/TASK_TIMEOUT=86400/' launch/miles-diffusion-wan/run.env","reader_overview":"Wan2.2 learns to produce videos using a fixed preference scorer that rewards the generated results. The agent adds the required video-training method to the Miles Diffusion training framework, then speeds up reward calculation, model training and transfer of updated weights.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Model and data","description":"Wan2.2-TI2V-5B in text-to-video mode, supplied prompts and the HPSv3 preference scorer. The video transformer is trained; the text encoder and video decoder stay frozen."},{"name":"Training setup","description":"Three rounds of eight prompts, with eight videos per prompt. Each video has nine frames at 704 × 1280 pixels and uses ten sampling steps; each round makes two training updates."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A Miles Diffusion framework with unfinished task commands and no DanceGRPO video-training support."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"The required video sampling behavior, preference scoring and learning updates, plus batching, memory placement and transfer of the complete updated model."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures how many fixed training replays can be completed per hour. The real trained model must retain a mean HPSv3 reward of at least the baseline minus 0.28; the replay duration determines the score.","rule":"Score = 1 ÷ median replay duration in hours."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB).","launch_note":"This launcher uses the same GPU pair for Worker and Judge."},"grouped-fp8":{"task_id":"standalone_dynamic_grouped_fp8_h100_scratch_v3","directory":"tasks/latest/standalone-dynamic-grouped-fp8-h100-v3-scratch","launcher":"launch/standalone-fp8/run.sh","objective":"Implement grouped FP8 matrix multiplication for dynamically sized expert token batches, supporting both masked and prefix-sum storage layouts.","workspace":["Scratch: candidates/kernel_v001/submission.py is empty.","Implement both matrix-multiplication entry points in versioned Triton candidates and select the submission through FINAL_VERSION."],"requirements":["Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","PyTorch 2.8 / CUDA 12.9 / Triton task environment.","FP8 inputs, block scales, group boundaries, and BF16 output buffers."],"evaluation":"Measures effective TFLOPS across both layouts. Numerical correctness, complete writes, unchanged inputs, and memory safety are checked across scored and boundary cases.","result_intro":"Implement the matrix multiplication used to process variable token batches in mixture-of-experts inference, starting from empty Triton code.","metric_summary":"The score counts only valid expert rows and divides their matrix-multiplication work by the full synchronized call time. Five-trial median TFLOPS are combined geometrically across 32 cases, with equal weight for masked and packed layouts.","setting":"The synthetic workload follows DeepGEMM’s grouped FP8 benchmark and uses no model weights. Its 32 scored configurations combine masked and packed layouts, four expert-count/token-count regimes, and four matrix shapes, with fresh valid row counts in every trial. The supported domain includes 1–64 experts, zero-row groups, and widths up to 8192. Optimization covers tiling, scale access, and scheduling across these changing group sizes.","launch_setup":"nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,uuid --format=csv,noheader","launch_command":"WORK_GPU=0 JUDGE_GPU=1 launch/standalone-fp8/run.sh \\\n  --legacy-dual-gpu \\\n  --model MODEL_ID --effort max --hours 12 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","reader_overview":"Mixture-of-experts inference sends different numbers of tokens to different expert networks, creating many matrix multiplications of uneven size. The agent builds a GPU implementation that processes these changing groups efficiently.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Workload","description":"Generated matrix data follows the DeepGEMM grouped FP8 workload. Expert batches vary in size and use either padded storage or a packed arrangement."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"Evaluation times 32 configurations across the two storage arrangements, expert counts, token counts, and matrix sizes."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"An empty implementation stub with public test tools and a written arithmetic contract."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"The Triton kernels that multiply each expert’s matrices, apply the supplied scales, and schedule work across groups."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures matrix-multiplication throughput using only actual expert rows. Masked and packed layouts have equal weight in the final score.","rule":"Score = useful matrix operations ÷ execution time, expressed in TFLOPS and combined across cases."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB)."},"sparse-mla":{"task_id":"standalone_sparse_mla_prefill_h100_scratch_v2","directory":"tasks/latest/standalone-sparse-mla-prefill-h100-v2-scratch","launcher":"launch/standalone-sparse-mla-v2/run.sh","objective":"Implement BF16 sparse MLA prefill: gather indexed keys, compute attention with the specified sink behavior, and return attention outputs and logit statistics.","workspace":["Scratch: candidates/kernel_v001/submission.py starts empty.","Implement gather, QK, softmax, and PV in versioned Triton candidates; select the submission through FINAL_VERSION."],"requirements":["Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","PyTorch 2.8 / CUDA 12.9 / Triton task environment.","BF16 Q/KV tensors, sparse indices, and sink/length inputs for the three MLA geometries."],"evaluation":"Measures effective throughput across three MLA geometry families. Reference output/statistics checks, input preservation, and memory-safety gates cover the supported domain.","result_intro":"Implement sparse multi-head latent attention for prefill from an empty Triton candidate.","metric_summary":"Throughput counts the selected query–key interactions, including QK and value aggregation, over the complete synchronized call. The Judge takes seven-trial medians for 13 cases and geometrically combines the three DeepSeek families with equal weight, measuring sparse-attention TFLOPS.","setting":"The scored workloads use DeepSeek V3.2, V4-Flash, and V4-Pro head geometries with 4096 query positions. Selected-key counts are 2048, 512, and 1024 respectively, while KV context lengths extend to 128K. Inputs are synthetic BF16 tensors with one shared KV head and a 512-dimensional value vector. Additional cases exercise arbitrary valid and invalid indices, optional sinks, variable selected-set lengths, and much larger query dimensions.","launch_setup":"nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,uuid --format=csv,noheader","launch_command":"WORK_GPU=0 JUDGE_GPU=1 launch/standalone-sparse-mla-v2/run.sh \\\n  --legacy-dual-gpu \\\n  --model MODEL_ID --effort max --hours 12 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","reader_overview":"Sparse attention computes each query’s output from a selected list of context positions, reducing the number of attention interactions. The agent builds a GPU implementation that gathers those positions and computes the output faster.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Workload","description":"Generated attention inputs use the head arrangements of DeepSeek V3.2, V4 Flash, and V4 Pro. Context lengths reach 128K tokens, and the number of selected positions differs across model families."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"Evaluation times 13 configurations across three model families."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"An empty attention implementation stub with public tests and the required selection rules."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"The Triton implementation for reading selected keys, computing attention, and producing the required normalization statistics."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures the speed of attention over the selected keys. The three DeepSeek workload families have equal weight.","rule":"Score = operations for selected query–key pairs ÷ execution time, expressed in TFLOPS and combined across cases."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB)."},"paged-gqa":{"task_id":"standalone_paged_gqa_decode_bf16_h100_scratch_v2","directory":"tasks/latest/standalone-paged-gqa-decode-bf16-h100-v2-scratch","launcher":"launch/standalone-paged-gqa-v2/run.sh","objective":"Implement BF16 grouped-query decode attention over paged KV caches, correctly applying page mappings, request lengths, and suffix-aligned causal masks.","workspace":["Scratch: candidates/kernel_v001/submission.py starts empty.","Implement cache gathering, masks, softmax, and accumulation in a versioned Triton candidate; select it through FINAL_VERSION."],"requirements":["Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","PyTorch 2.8 / CUDA 12.9 / Triton task environment.","BF16 queries, physical KV pages, page tables, and per-request lengths."],"evaluation":"Measures effective attention throughput for single-query and short multi-query workloads. Numerical references, complete output writes, input preservation, and memory safety must pass.","result_intro":"Implement grouped-query attention over a paged KV cache for single-token decode and short-query prefill.","metric_summary":"The score divides the QK and value-aggregation work for causally visible pairs by synchronized call time, including page lookup and softmax. Seven-trial median TFLOPS are geometrically aggregated across five head configurations in each of the equally weighted one-query and 16-query families.","setting":"The ten scored cases pair query lengths 1 and 16 with head configurations from Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen-2.5-7B, Llama-3.1-70B, Mistral Large, and Llama-3.1-405B at TP8. The operator uses synthetic BF16 queries and caches with head dimension 128 and request lengths up to 32768. The broader support domain includes shared physical pages, oversized page tables, and arbitrary query lengths from 1 to 16. Model weights and text generation are outside this operator workload.","launch_setup":"nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,uuid --format=csv,noheader","launch_command":"WORK_GPU=0 JUDGE_GPU=1 launch/standalone-paged-gqa-v2/run.sh \\\n  --legacy-dual-gpu \\\n  --model MODEL_ID --effort max --hours 12 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","reader_overview":"Language model serving stores earlier attention keys and values in memory pages that may be scattered across the GPU. The agent builds a faster attention operation that follows those page mappings, shares cached data across query heads, and respects which earlier tokens each query may read.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Workload","description":"Generated queries and paged caches represent five deployed-model head arrangements: Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen-2.5-7B, Llama-3.1-70B, Mistral Large, and Llama-3.1-405B."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"Ten timed configurations combine the five head arrangements with one-token and 16-token queries."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"An empty paged-attention implementation stub with public tests and page-addressing rules."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"The Triton kernels that locate cached keys and values, compute attention, and schedule work across requests and query heads."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures attention throughput for the query–key pairs allowed by the causal mask. Timing includes page lookup and the full attention call. Single-query and 16-query workloads have equal weight.","rule":"Score = useful attention operations ÷ execution time, expressed in TFLOPS and combined across cases."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB)."},"sageattention":{"task_id":"sageattention_quantized_attention_bf16_h100_seed_v2","directory":"tasks/latest/sageattention-quantized-attention-bf16-h100-v2-seed","launcher":"launch/sageattention-qattn/run.sh","objective":"Accelerate the complete attention forward call for BF16 inputs and outputs, using quantized intermediate computation within the specified accuracy limits.","workspace":["Seed: candidates/kernel_v001/submission.py is a self-contained SageAttention-1 Triton implementation.","Edit versioned submission.py files, then select the submitted version with FINAL_VERSION."],"requirements":["Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","PyTorch 2.8 / CUDA 12.9 / Triton task environment.","Synthetic workloads and real-layer fixtures from CogVideoX-2B and Llama-3.1-8B."],"evaluation":"Measures dense-equivalent attention throughput after output-accuracy and input-integrity checks. Quantization and preprocessing remain part of the timed call.","result_intro":"Accelerate an attention forward pass from BF16 Q, K, and V to a BF16 output, starting from a working SageAttention-style Triton implementation.","metric_summary":"The score uses dense attention work as a common numerator, while timing the entire candidate pipeline, including smoothing and quantization. Seven-trial median TFLOPS are combined geometrically across three video-DiT and two causal-LLM cells, with each family receiving half the weight.","setting":"The seed computes key means, blockwise INT8 Q/K quantization, online softmax, and FP16 probability–value products. Five scored cells span noncausal video attention and causal language-model attention with head widths 64 or 128. Synthetic inputs vary token scales and channel offsets to expose quantization errors. Accuracy checks also use eight edge fixtures and captured activations from all 30 CogVideoX-2B layers and 32 Llama-3.1-8B layers.","launch_setup":"nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,uuid --format=csv,noheader","launch_command":"WORK_GPU=0 JUDGE_GPU=1 launch/sageattention-qattn/run.sh \\\n  --legacy-dual-gpu \\\n  --model MODEL_ID --effort max --hours 12 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","reader_overview":"Attention can run faster when intermediate values use fewer bits, but this introduces numerical error. The agent improves a working quantized-attention implementation, speeding up the complete operation from input preprocessing through output generation.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Workload","description":"Generated inputs cover video attention and causal language-model attention. Additional accuracy checks use activations captured from CogVideoX-2B and Llama-3.1-8B."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"Five timed configurations comprise three video-attention and two causal-attention cases."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A working SageAttention-style implementation that smooths and quantizes inputs before computing attention."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"Quantization precision and granularity, fused processing steps, and GPU scheduling within the Triton attention implementation."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures the full attention pipeline, including smoothing and quantization, using dense attention work as the common operation count. Video and language-model workloads have equal weight.","rule":"Score = dense-equivalent attention operations ÷ execution time, expressed in TFLOPS and combined across cases."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB)."},"minference":{"task_id":"minference_sparse_prefill_h100_seed_v3","directory":"tasks/latest/minference-sparse-prefill-h100-v3-seed","launcher":"launch/minference-v3/run.sh","objective":"Accelerate one long-context causal-attention call by choosing an efficient sparse or dense computation while staying within the fixed output-error budget.","workspace":["Seed: candidates/kernel_v001/submission.py includes the MInference vertical/slash estimator, sparse Triton kernels, and error-based dense-head repair.","Edit the estimator, sparse structure, budgets, indexing, and kernels in versioned candidates; select the version with FINAL_VERSION."],"requirements":["Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","PyTorch 2.8 / CUDA 12.9 environment with Triton, Transformers, and FlashAttention.","Llama-3.1-8B and RULER prompts for generating layer Q/K/V at multiple context lengths."],"evaluation":"Measures dense-equivalent throughput across the prescribed text/length/layer workloads. Every timed call and boundary case must satisfy the vector-error and integrity checks.","result_intro":"Optimize one long-context causal attention call on real Llama-3.1-8B activations, using a MInference-style sparse seed.","metric_summary":"The score divides full dense-causal attention work by the time for pattern estimation, index conversion, attention, repairs, and synchronized output handling. It geometrically averages 48 cell throughputs, each a median over three prompt trials, so the reported TFLOPS measure equivalent kernel work.","setting":"Inputs are captured from Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct on four RULER task types: single-needle retrieval, multi-key retrieval, variable tracking, and frequent-word extraction. Each type is evaluated at 32K, 64K, and 128K context budgets and layers 1, 4, 15, and 27. Three prompts per cell produce 144 timed attention calls; their actual lengths remain below the context budget. Model execution prepares Q/K/V outside the measured candidate call.","launch_setup":"","launch_command":"launch/minference-v3/run.sh \\\n  --work-gpu 0 --judge-gpu 1 \\\n  --model MODEL_ID --effort max --hours 12 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","reader_overview":"Long prompts make attention expensive because each token can attend to many earlier tokens. The agent improves a sparse attention implementation that processes fewer interactions, reducing computation while keeping its outputs close to full attention on real model activations.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Workload","description":"Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct produces the attention inputs from RULER benchmark prompts for single-needle retrieval, multi-key retrieval, variable tracking, and frequent-word extraction."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"Four prompt types, three context budgets of 32K, 64K, and 128K, and four model layers form 48 evaluation cells. Three prompts per cell produce 144 timed calls."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A working sparse-attention seed that estimates attention patterns and uses dense computation to repair heads when needed."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"Pattern selection, the amount of sparse computation, indexing, GPU kernels, and the repair strategy within the fixed output-error limits."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures how much full-attention work the faster implementation replaces per second. Timing includes selecting the sparse pattern and any accuracy-repair computation. Results are combined across 48 task, length, and layer settings.","rule":"Score = dense-equivalent attention operations ÷ full call time, expressed in TFLOPS and combined across cases."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB)."},"lmcache":{"task_id":"lmcache_paged_kv_block_transfer_h100_seed_v1","directory":"tasks/latest/lmcache-paged-kv-block-transfer-h100-v1-seed","launcher":"launch/lmcache-kv-transfer/run.sh","objective":"Accelerate exact KV-cache transfers between an inference engine’s paged layout and LMCache’s contiguous chunks, including GPU and mapped-host memory paths.","workspace":["Seed: candidates/kernel_v001/submission.cu ports the upstream multi_layer_block_kv_transfer kernel.","Edit one CUDA C++17 translation unit per candidate, candidates/kernel_vNNN/submission.cu.","Select the submission with FINAL_VERSION; record experiments in notes.md."],"requirements":["Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","At least 16 GiB of pinnable host memory for mapped-host transfers.","PyTorch 2.8 / CUDA 12.9 build environment and KV-transfer descriptors."],"evaluation":"Measures payload bandwidth across device, host, and trace workloads. Moved elements must match bit for bit, while source and uncovered destination regions remain unchanged.","result_intro":"Accelerate KV-cache movement between an inference engine’s paged buffers and LMCache’s contiguous chunks using the supplied CUDA seed.","metric_summary":"Each case measures logical KV payload bytes divided by the median of five synchronized transfer durations. Geometric group bandwidths receive weights 0.55 for device transfers, 0.15 for host transfers, and 0.30 for mixed-call traces; each required payload transfer is counted once.","setting":"The workload moves whole 16-token blocks in store and retrieve directions across multiple layers and heads. It includes split K/V and fused layouts, 1–4 chunk objects per call, arbitrary unique block mappings, and skipped prefixes. Device cases keep chunks on the GPU, while host and trace cases use mapped pinned host memory accessed over PCIe. Twenty scored cases combine 16 direct transfers and four traces whose shared engine-pool state evolves across calls.","launch_setup":"nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,uuid --format=csv,noheader","launch_command":"WORK_GPU=0 JUDGE_GPU=1 launch/lmcache-kv-transfer/run.sh \\\n  --legacy-dual-gpu \\\n  --model MODEL_ID --effort max --hours 12 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","reader_overview":"Language model serving reuses cached attention data, which must move between paged engine buffers and contiguous cache storage. The agent improves a GPU transfer kernel to move this data faster across device and host-memory paths.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Workload","description":"Generated cache buffers exercise transfers in both store and retrieve directions. Cases use different block layouts and mappings, with cache storage on the GPU or in host memory accessible to the GPU."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"Twenty timed cases include 16 direct transfers and four sequences of transfers that update shared cache state."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A working CUDA transfer kernel based on the supplied LMCache implementation."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"How the CUDA kernel divides, groups, and orders data transfers, including access patterns for host memory; the transfer interface remains fixed."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures logical KV-cache data moved per second. The final score combines device, host, and mixed-transfer workloads with weights of 55%, 15%, and 30%.","rule":"Per-case bandwidth = transferred gigabytes ÷ transfer seconds; score = weighted geometric mean of group bandwidths."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB)."},"flash-kmeans":{"task_id":"flash_kmeans_paper_h100_kernel_lab_v7_scratch","directory":"tasks/latest/flash-kmeans-paper-h100-v7-scratch","launcher":"launch/flash-kmeans/run.sh","objective":"Accelerate one GPU K-Means iteration: assign every point to its nearest centroid, then compute counts and updated centroids while retaining old centroids for empty clusters.","workspace":["Scratch: start in the empty kernels/ directory and create the first candidate and version selector.","Create kernels/flash_kmeans_vNNN.py implementing assign and update; PyTorch and Triton are supported.","Create FINAL_VERSION to choose the submitted implementation; use notes.md for experiments."],"requirements":["Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB.","PyTorch 2.7.1 / CUDA 12.8 task environment.","GPU-resident points, centroids, and precomputed point norms."],"evaluation":"The Judge measures weighted workload latency after assignment, count, centroid, input-integrity, and memory checks. The website presents its documented equivalent-throughput conversion.","result_intro":"Implement one exact Euclidean K-Means iteration from an empty GPU workspace: assign each point to its nearest centroid, then update counts and centroid means.","metric_summary":"The Judge measures weighted geometric-mean latency across 14 workloads. The paper and website express this as equivalent TFLOPS using the workloads’ fixed operation counts.","setting":"Synthetic batched points and centroids are already on the GPU, with precomputed squared point norms supplied by the caller. The implementation performs a single Lloyd update using PyTorch and/or Triton. Fourteen scored workloads cover assignment, update, full iterations, fresh-process control, large-K, and large-N/high-dimension groups. Eight additional correctness cases test ties, empty clusters, and numerical boundaries; the task uses no model weights or external dataset.","launch_setup":"nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,uuid --format=csv,noheader","launch_command":"WORK_GPU=0 JUDGE_GPU=1 launch/flash-kmeans/run.sh \\\n  --legacy-dual-gpu \\\n  --model MODEL_ID --effort max --hours 12 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","reader_overview":"One K-Means iteration assigns every point to its nearest cluster center and then updates each center from its assigned points. The agent implements these two operations efficiently on the GPU.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Workload","description":"Generated batches of points and cluster centers are already on the GPU. The workload measures one clustering iteration with varying numbers of points, centers, and features."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"Fourteen timed workloads cover assignment, center updates, complete iterations, fresh-process runs, and large workloads."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"An empty implementation workspace with public tests for both clustering operations."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"The PyTorch or Triton implementations of nearest-center assignment and center updates, including how distances and reductions are divided across the GPU."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"The Judge measures execution latency across 14 workloads. The website converts that latency to equivalent TFLOPS using a fixed amount of work.","rule":"Displayed score = fixed workload operation count ÷ weighted geometric-mean latency, expressed in TFLOPS."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU. H100 NVL (94 GB)."},"mamba2":{"task_id":"tilelang_mamba2_ssd_forward_h100_seed_v2","directory":"tasks/latest/tilelang-mamba2-ssd-forward-h100-v2-seed","launcher":"launch/tilelang-mamba2-ssd-v2/run.sh","objective":"Accelerate the Mamba-2 state-space forward operator while preserving the specified recurrent state update and BF16 output.","workspace":["Seed: candidates/kernel_v001/submission.py supplies a correct four-stage TileLang implementation.","Implement ssd_forward(x, dt, A, B, C, D, out) in the versioned candidate.","Tune TileLang computation and buffer layouts; select the submission through FINAL_VERSION."],"requirements":["Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","PyTorch 2.8 / CUDA 12.9 with TileLang 0.1.13.","State-space tensors with Mamba-2, Codestral, and Falcon-H1 geometries."],"evaluation":"Measures recurrence-equivalent throughput across three model families. FP32-reference tolerances, complete finite outputs, unchanged inputs, and temporary-memory limits must pass.","result_intro":"Accelerate the Mamba-2 state-space forward operator from a correct four-stage TileLang seed.","metric_summary":"The score counts recurrence-equivalent state-update and output-projection work over the full synchronized forward call. Seven-trial median TFLOPS are geometrically averaged over six lengths in each of three equally weighted model families.","setting":"The frozen synthetic workload uses head and state geometries from Mamba-2-2.7B, Mamba-Codestral-7B, and Falcon-H1-34B. Each family runs sequence lengths from 1024 to 32768, normally at batch eight; the longest Codestral case uses batch four. The seed splits decay accumulation, chunk-state formation, state passing, and chunk scanning into separate kernels with chunk length 256. Eighteen additional fixtures exercise short sequences, ragged tails, extreme decays, and isolated recurrence terms.","launch_setup":"nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,uuid --format=csv,noheader","launch_command":"WORK_GPU=0 JUDGE_GPU=1 launch/tilelang-mamba2-ssd-v2/run.sh \\\n  --legacy-dual-gpu \\\n  --model MODEL_ID --effort max --hours 12 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","reader_overview":"State-space models carry a compact state through a sequence to compute each token’s output. The agent speeds up this forward operation by improving how work is divided and intermediate states are stored.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Workload","description":"Generated sequence inputs use the state and head arrangements of Mamba-2-2.7B, Mamba-Codestral-7B, and Falcon-H1-34B."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"Each of the three model families has six timed sequence lengths from 1K to 32K tokens, giving 18 cases."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A correct TileLang implementation that divides the forward computation into four stages."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"Sequence chunking, intermediate storage, GPU layouts, stage fusion, and scheduling inside the TileLang computation."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures sequence-processing speed using the prescribed state-update and output-projection work. All work in the forward call is timed, and the three model families have equal weight.","rule":"Score = recurrence-equivalent operations ÷ execution time, expressed in TFLOPS and combined across cases."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB)."},"kvpress":{"task_id":"kvpress_kv_eviction_policy_qwen3_8b_h100_scratch_v2","directory":"tasks/latest/kvpress-kv-eviction-policy-qwen3-8b-h100-v2-scratch","launcher":"launch/kvpress-kv-eviction-scratch/run.sh","objective":"Design a post-prefill KV eviction policy for Qwen3-8B that retains useful cache positions while preserving the model’s next-token distribution.","workspace":["Scratch task with a positional baseline: candidates/policy_v001/ keeps sink and recent positions.","Implement EvictionPolicy in candidates/policy_vNNN/submission.py, including select and optional layer_budgets.","Optional policy assets are supported within the documented size limit."],"requirements":["Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 7 separate GPUs.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","Frozen Qwen3-8B and the supplied retrieval, aggregation, QA, and generation inputs."],"evaluation":"Measures next-token distribution fidelity at three eviction fractions. Selection validity, cache budgets, input preservation, determinism, selection time, and temporary memory are checked.","result_intro":"Design a policy that chooses which Qwen3-8B KV-cache positions to retain after prefill.","metric_summary":"The score measures output-distribution fidelity on a 0–100 scale. Each item’s mean token-level KL divergence is converted to fidelity; item means are combined geometrically across four task families, then averaged across the three eviction fractions. Identical distributions score 100.","setting":"The frozen BF16 Qwen3-8B model has 36 layers, 32 query heads, and 8 KV heads. Selection happens after context prefill, before the downstream question is appended, at eviction fractions of 50%, 75%, and 87.5%. The private suite contains 5,000 contexts of 6K–28K tokens from RULER, LongBench, LooGLE, and Zero-SCROLLS, covering retrieval, aggregation, question answering, and generation. Trusted replay measures both distributions on the same full-cache reference continuation.","launch_command":"launch/kvpress-kv-eviction-scratch/run.sh \\\n  --model MODEL_ID \\\n  --effort max \\\n  --hours 24 \\\n  --work-gpu 0 \\\n  --judge-gpus 1,2,3,4,5,6,7 \\\n  --judge-port 18621 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","launch_setup":"","reader_overview":"Language models retain information from the input text in a memory called the KV cache, which can become expensive for long passages. The agent designs a rule for removing cache entries while preserving the model’s predictions about the next token.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Model and data","description":"Qwen3-8B with long-context tasks from RULER, LongBench, LooGLE, and Zero-SCROLLS, covering retrieval, aggregation, question answering, and generation."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"The rule removes 50%, 75%, or 87.5% of cached positions before the question is supplied. Public tests contain 85 examples; formal evaluation uses 5,000 passages of 6K–28K tokens."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A simple baseline keeps the first four positions and the most recent positions in the cache."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"The rule for choosing retained positions and distributing the available cache space across model layers. The model and the evaluator’s generation process remain fixed."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures how closely the compressed cache preserves the model’s next-token probabilities. A score of 100 means identical distributions. The score combines four task families at three eviction settings.","rule":"Score = 100 × average across three eviction settings of the geometric mean of four family fidelities."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 7 separate GPUs. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB)."},"kvzip":{"task_id":"kvzip_query_agnostic_kv_scoring_qwen3_8b_h100_scratch_v2","directory":"tasks/latest/kvzip-query-agnostic-kv-scoring-qwen3-8b-h100-v2-scratch","launcher":"launch/kvzip-kv-scoring/run.sh","objective":"Assign query-agnostic importance scores to Qwen3-8B context KV entries so compressed caches retain downstream answer quality before the question is known.","workspace":["Scratch: candidates/scorer_v001/submission.py is empty.","Implement KVScorer.score in candidates/scorer_vNNN/submission.py, producing comparable FP32 scores across layers and heads.","Optional scorer assets are supported within the documented size limit."],"requirements":["Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 7 separate GPUs.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","Frozen Qwen3-8B and the supplied retrieval, contextual-QA, and redundancy workloads."],"evaluation":"Measures answer-quality retention relative to the full cache. Score shape/finite values, query isolation, deterministic behavior, unchanged inputs, and scoring-time/memory limits must pass.","result_intro":"Assign query-agnostic importance scores to Qwen3-8B context KV entries before the downstream question is supplied.","metric_summary":"The score averages compressed/full-cache answer-quality ratios within each dataset group, across the three groups, and across 10%, 20%, and 30% keep fractions, then multiplies by 100. Native answer scores are averaged over questions within each context row and then over rows; 100 denotes full-cache quality.","setting":"A frozen BF16 Qwen3-8B supplies context activations, queries, keys, values, and projection weights to the scorer. One set of importance scores serves all three keep fractions, with global selection across the model’s 36 layers and 8 KV heads. The private suite has 1,812 context rows and about 7,000 questions across 12 tasks in retrieval, contextual QA, and redundancy. System-prefix positions remain protected, and questions are appended only after scoring.","launch_command":"launch/kvzip-kv-scoring/run.sh \\\n  --model MODEL_ID \\\n  --effort max \\\n  --hours 24 \\\n  --work-gpu 0 \\\n  --judge-gpus 1,2,3,4,5,6,7 \\\n  --judge-port 18622 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","launch_setup":"","reader_overview":"A model may need to answer several questions about a document while keeping only a small part of its cached context. The agent assigns importance scores before any question is known, so the retained information supports accurate answers across different questions.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Model and data","description":"Qwen3-8B with SQuAD, GSM8K, SCBench, and RULER-based workloads covering retrieval, contextual question answering, and redundant context."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"The same importance scores select the top 10%, 20%, and 30% of context cache entries. Public tests contain 63 passages; formal evaluation has 1,812 passages and about 7,000 questions across 12 tasks."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"An empty importance-scoring implementation, with the model, test inputs, and evaluation tools supplied."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"How each cached entry is scored using information available from the document. The evaluator selects entries from those scores and runs the unchanged model on the questions."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures how much answer quality remains after cache compression, relative to the same model with its full cache. A score of 100 matches full-cache answer quality, averaged across dataset groups and the three memory budgets.","rule":"Score = 100 × average ratio of compressed-cache answer quality to full-cache answer quality."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 7 separate GPUs. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB)."},"qvg":{"task_id":"quant_videogen_cross_model_kv_codec_h100_scratch_v4","directory":"tasks/latest/quant-videogen-cross-model-kv-codec-h100-v4-scratch","launcher":"launch/quant-videogen/run.sh","objective":"Implement one persistent KV-cache codec for LongCat-Video and HY-WorldPlay that reduces storage while preserving acceptable generated-video quality and runtime.","workspace":["Scratch: submission/ starts empty.","Implement encode/decode in submission/mlsysforge_codec.py; optional helper modules may live under codec/.","Use notes.md to record compression, reconstruction, and video-quality experiments."],"requirements":["Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 6 separate GPUs (8 for GLM).","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB.","LongCat-Video, HY-WorldPlay-8B, and the supplied video-generation inputs.","PyTorch 2.8 / CUDA 12.8 runtime with video-quality evaluators; Judge supports 1–8 same-model GPUs.","Host: git-lfs on PATH and about 200 GiB free under INFRATHON_ASSETS_ROOT.","Before fetching assets, accept the FLUX.1-Redux-dev license and authenticate with hf auth login or HF_TOKEN."],"evaluation":"Measures persistent-cache compression across both models. Reconstructed-cache correctness, video fidelity/quality, complete compression coverage, runtime, and memory checks must pass.","result_intro":"Implement a persistent KV-cache codec that compresses CUDA BF16 key and value tensors and reconstructs them for video generation.","metric_summary":"Each model’s compression factor is total original BF16 bytes divided by total stored payload bytes across its cases and compression calls. The score is the geometric mean of the two model factors, including all reconstruction metadata.","setting":"The codec receives contiguous four-dimensional tensors in batch, head, sequence, and feature order. Formal evaluation covers six LongCat-Video continuation cases and eleven HY-WorldPlay image-conditioned camera-trajectory cases, using one implementation for both models. The public end-to-end workload instead uses Self-Forcing with Wan2.1-1.3B to test transfer on a third model. Frozen BF16 generations provide reconstruction and video-quality references, while the evaluator accounts for every persistent payload allocation.","launch_command":"launch/quant-videogen/run.sh \\\n  --model MODEL_ID \\\n  --effort max \\\n  --hours 24 \\\n  --work-gpu 0 \\\n  --judge-gpus 1,2,3,4,5,6 \\\n  --judge-port 18623 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","launch_setup":"","reader_overview":"Video generators store attention information between generation steps, consuming substantial memory as videos grow. The agent builds one compression and reconstruction method that reduces this stored information across two video models while maintaining video quality and acceptable generation time.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Model and data","description":"Formal evaluation uses LongCat-Video continuations and HY-WorldPlay image-conditioned videos with camera movement. Public end-to-end tests use Self-Forcing with Wan2.1-1.3B."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"The same method runs on six LongCat-Video cases and eleven HY-WorldPlay cases. Generated videos are checked against uncompressed references, and stored size includes everything needed for reconstruction."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"An empty submission, plus tests for compression correctness and public video generation."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"How attention information is packed into a smaller representation and reconstructed for later use. The video models and generation workloads remain fixed."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures persistent KV-cache storage reduction across both video models. All reconstruction metadata counts toward stored size. A score of 1 means no compression.","rule":"Per-model compression = original BF16 bytes ÷ stored bytes; score = geometric mean of the two model factors."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 6 separate GPUs (8 for GLM). H100 NVL (94 GB)."},"eagle3":{"task_id":"eagle3_spec_decode_engine_qwen3_8b_h100_v1","directory":"tasks/latest/eagle3-spec-decode-engine-qwen3-8b-h100-v1-seed","launcher":"launch/eagle3-spec-decode/run.sh","objective":"Accelerate complete speculative generation using a fixed Qwen3-8B target and EAGLE-3 draft head, including tree construction, verification, acceptance, and KV-cache updates.","workspace":["Seed: solutions/engine_v001/ ports the upstream eagenerate execution path.","Edit versioned solutions/engine_vNNN/ implementations and choose the submission with FINAL_VERSION.","The engine can change tree strategy, batching, kernels, cache layout, compilation, and buffer reuse."],"requirements":["Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","Fixed Qwen3-8B target, EAGLE-3 draft head, and the supplied generation prompts.","PyTorch 2.8 / CUDA 12.9, Transformers 4.53.1, Triton, and nvcc."],"evaluation":"Measures generated-token throughput across six workload cells. A fresh target checks calibrated greedy consistency; stopping rules, request isolation, determinism, execution, and resource checks must pass.","result_intro":"Accelerate the complete EAGLE-3 speculative-decoding engine for a frozen Qwen3-8B target and draft head.","metric_summary":"Each workload cell pools emitted tokens and synchronized generation seconds, including prefill and an emitted EOS. The final score is the equally weighted geometric mean of six cell throughputs in tokens/s.","setting":"The task supplies frozen BF16 Qwen3-8B target weights and an EAGLE-3 draft head, plus a working speculative-decoding seed. Six workload cells cover chat, math, code, long context, thinking-mode math, and mixed batches, using MT-Bench, GSM8K, HumanEval, and UltraChat prompts. Calls contain one to eight requests with cell-specific output budgets of 256, 512, or 1,024 tokens. A fresh target checks each candidate’s emitted trajectory under calibrated numerical tolerances.","launch_command":"WORK_GPU=0 JUDGE_GPU=1 launch/eagle3-spec-decode/run.sh \\\n  --legacy-dual-gpu \\\n  --model MODEL_ID --effort max --hours 24 \\\n  --judge-port 18386 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","launch_setup":"nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,uuid --format=csv,noheader","reader_overview":"Speculative decoding uses a small drafting component to propose several tokens for a larger model to check together. The agent accelerates this complete proposal-and-check process.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Model and data","description":"A fixed Qwen3-8B target and EAGLE-3 drafting component, with prompts from MT-Bench, GSM8K, HumanEval, and UltraChat."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"Six workloads cover chat, math, code, long context, extended math reasoning, and mixed batches. Calls contain one to eight requests, with output limits of 256, 512, or 1,024 tokens."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A working EAGLE-3 engine that proposes candidate tokens, verifies them, accepts predictions, and updates stored attention information."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"The proposal strategy, request batching, computation, memory layout, and reuse of working buffers. Generated tokens must pass consistency checks against the fixed target model."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures generated tokens per second over complete generation calls, including prompt processing. The final score gives equal weight to the six workloads.","rule":"Per-workload throughput = generated tokens ÷ generation seconds; score = geometric mean of six throughputs."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB)."},"llada":{"task_id":"llada_h100_decoder_optimization_v2","directory":"tasks/latest/llada-h100-decoder-optimization-v2-vanilla-seed","launcher":"launch/llada-h100/run.sh","objective":"Accelerate the LLaDA-8B-Instruct masked-token decoding engine while retaining useful completions and benchmark answer quality.","workspace":["Seed: solutions/decoder_v001/candidate.py provides the vanilla masked-token decoder.","Edit candidate.py and __init__.py in solutions/decoder_vNNN/; optimize engine.generate().","Choose the submission through FINAL_VERSION and document experiments in notes.md."],"requirements":["Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","LLaDA-8B-Instruct, its tokenizer, and GSM8K, MATH, HumanEval, and MBPP workloads.","PyTorch 2.7.1 / CUDA 12.8 / Transformers 4.49 task environment."],"evaluation":"Measures useful-token throughput across twelve workload rows. Reference-relative answer quality, useful-token retention, determinism, request isolation, model integrity, time, and memory checks must pass.","result_intro":"Accelerate the iterative masked-token decoder for frozen LLaDA-8B-Instruct.","metric_summary":"Pool useful completion tokens and complete decoding time within each workload row, then take the geometric mean of all twelve row throughputs in tokens/s. Useful-token counting follows benchmark-specific stop rules; frozen-reference quality and token-retention checks determine whether the throughput is accepted.","setting":"The task runs frozen BF16 LLaDA-8B-Instruct with batch size one and a 4,096-token context limit. Its twelve workload rows combine GSM8K, MATH, HumanEval, and MBPP with few-shot settings and output budgets of 256, 512, or 1,024 tokens. Formal evaluation contains 520 generations, with 130 targets per benchmark. The supplied decoder fills masked positions iteratively, and the candidate may change decoding control and request-local reuse while preserving model weights and the output contract.","launch_command":"WORK_GPU=0 JUDGE_GPU=1 launch/llada-h100/run.sh \\\n  --legacy-dual-gpu \\\n  --model MODEL_ID --effort max --hours 24 \\\n  --judge-port 18384 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","launch_setup":"export LLADA_MODEL_REPO=\"$(.venv/bin/python scripts/infrathon/assets.py hub-cache)/models--GSAI-ML--LLaDA-8B-Instruct\"\nnvidia-smi -L\npython3 tasks/latest/llada-h100-decoder-optimization-v2-vanilla-seed/scripts/make_reference_archives.py --task-root tasks/latest/llada-h100-decoder-optimization-v2-vanilla-seed --stress-extra-mib 61440","reader_overview":"LLaDA generates text by repeatedly filling missing positions in an output sequence using a diffusion language model. The agent speeds up this iterative process through better scheduling and reuse of computation while preserving useful completions and answer quality on math and coding tasks.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Model and data","description":"Frozen LLaDA-8B-Instruct evaluated on GSM8K, MATH, HumanEval, and MBPP."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"Formal evaluation contains 520 generations across twelve settings, with 130 targets per benchmark and output limits of 256, 512, or 1,024 tokens. Requests run individually, and quality is compared with a fixed reference decoder."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A basic decoder that repeatedly runs the model and fills masked output positions, plus public interface and generation tests."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"The decoding schedule, selection of positions to fill, and computation reused within each request. Model weights and the model’s forward computation remain fixed."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures useful completion tokens per second, with counting rules for each benchmark’s stopping behavior. Quality and output-length retention are checked against the frozen reference decoder.","rule":"Per-workload throughput = useful completion tokens ÷ decoding seconds; score = geometric mean of twelve throughputs."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 1 GPU; Judge: 1 separate GPU. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB)."},"sdv2":{"task_id":"streamdiffusionv2_dual_h100_pipeline_runtime_seed_v2","directory":"tasks/latest/streamdiffusionv2-dual-h100-pipeline-runtime-v2-seed","launcher":"launch/sdv2-pipeline/run.sh","objective":"Improve live video-to-video throughput and tail latency by scheduling StreamDiffusionV2 stages across two GPUs while preserving identical stream outputs.","workspace":["Seed: runtime_v001 supplies a working two-GPU runtime with 15/15 DiT-block placement.","Edit candidates/runtime_vNNN/ to implement the Runtime start, push, flush, and close lifecycle.","Optimize placement, communication, complete KV-state migration, buffers, and stream/graph scheduling."],"requirements":["Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","At least 64 GiB usable host RAM per container.","PyTorch 2.6 / CUDA 12.4 runtime, frozen StreamDiffusionV2 computation, video chunks, and prompt embeddings.","Host: CUDA-enabled PyTorch in the python3 first on PATH for the GPU-pair preflight."],"evaluation":"Measures latency-weighted streaming FPS. Every latent and decoded frame must match the seed bit for bit; ordering, causal execution, latency, construction, memory, and cleanup checks must pass.","result_intro":"Optimize a live video-to-video runtime around frozen StreamDiffusionV2 computation on two GPUs.","metric_summary":"For each stream, multiply steady-state FPS by the fourth root of the seed-to-candidate p99 latency ratio. The score is the geometric mean across twelve streams, retaining FPS units; throughput and push-to-emit latency use the same post-warm-up chunks.","setting":"The frozen StreamDiffusionV2 pipeline uses Wan2.1-T2V-1.3B with 30 DiT blocks, VAE encoding and decoding, and persistent stream state. The supplied runtime splits the blocks 15/15 across two GPUs, then processes four-frame chunks after a five-frame preparation chunk. Six public cases vary resolution, stream length, and two or four denoising steps; twelve private cases extend these settings to one through four steps. Stage placement, communication, overlap, and complete state migration are candidate-controlled.","launch_command":"JUDGE_PORT=18612 launch/sdv2-pipeline/run.sh \\\n  --model MODEL_ID \\\n  --effort max \\\n  --hours 24 \\\n  --gpus 0,1 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","launch_setup":"","reader_overview":"Live video transformation must process incoming frames quickly without allowing delays to build up. The agent improves how StreamDiffusionV2 schedules model stages, moves data, and reuses memory, increasing sustained output speed.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Model and data","description":"StreamDiffusionV2 with the fixed Wan2.1-T2V-1.3B model, prompt embeddings, and incoming video streams."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"Processing starts with five preparation frames and continues in four-frame chunks. Six public and twelve formal cases vary resolution, stream length, prompts, and the number of refinement steps used to generate each output."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A working pipeline with fixed placement of model stages and ordered processing of video chunks."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"Where stages run, how computation and transfers overlap, and how working memory and persistent stream state are managed. Model computation and output frames must remain unchanged."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Balances video frame rate with tail latency. The latency term uses the delay below which 99% of chunks finish.","rule":"Per-stream score = FPS × fourth root of (reference tail latency ÷ candidate tail latency); score = geometric mean across streams."},"reader_compute":"Worker: 2 GPUs; Judge: 2 GPUs on a separate NVLink pair. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB).","launch_note":"This launcher uses the same GPU pair for Worker and Judge."},"serving":{"task_id":"concurrent_agent_serving_optimizer_qwen36_swe192_v24","directory":"tasks/latest/concurrent-agent-serving-optimizer-qwen36-swe192-v24","launcher":"launch/concurrent-serving/run.sh","objective":"Optimize a transparent scheduling gateway for 192 concurrent coding-agent workflows, increasing completed valid tool steps on the fixed Qwen3.6-27B backend.","workspace":["Seed: submission/ contains the V3.11 program-aware admission gateway.","Edit gateway.py and the gateway implementation while preserving the launch.sh service interface.","Read the supplied RUNTIME_CONTRACT.json and submission/ARCHITECTURE.md for the serving contract and starter design."],"requirements":["Worker and Judge run on CPUs; the model backend uses 2 dedicated GPUs.","Release hardware: H100 NVL 94 GB or H100 SXM 80 GB.","Qwen3.6-27B served by vLLM 0.24.0 and 192 SWE-bench Lite tool environments.","Disk: about 230 GB for SWE-bench images, 55.6 GB for model files, and 30 GB for the backend image.","Host tools: Docker with Compose v2, the repository virtual environment, jq, and flock.","A CONNECT proxy at 127.0.0.1:18080 that reaches the agent model API.","Hidden and public Direct baselines measured on the current backend boot, plus a registered candidate-history store."],"evaluation":"Measures valid executable tool steps per minute across all workflows. Protocol transparency, one-to-one forwarding, request success, valid-step proportion, waiting, backend work per step, and accounting checks must pass.","result_intro":"Optimize an OpenAI-compatible scheduling gateway between concurrent coding agents and a fixed Qwen3.6-27B backend.","metric_summary":"The score is the total number of valid executable steps completed across all workflows during an exact 1,800-second window, divided by 30 minutes. A completed tool return counts regardless of its exit code.","setting":"The workload runs 192 distinct SWE-bench Lite coding agents concurrently, each capped at 100 steps. Agents alternate between model requests and tool execution against one fixed vLLM 0.24.0 backend serving Qwen3.6-27B with tensor parallelism across two GPUs. Work and Judge are CPU-only; the candidate changes the gateway while the model server and agent environments remain fixed. Formal evaluation uses 300 seconds of warm-up followed by a 1,800-second scored window.","launch_command":"launch/concurrent-serving/run.sh \\\n  --config launch/concurrent-serving/run.env \\\n  --model MODEL_ID \\\n  --effort max \\\n  --hours 24 \\\n  --auth /path/to/auth.json","launch_setup":"","reader_overview":"Many coding agents share one model server while alternating between generating actions and running tools. The agent builds a scheduling gateway that decides which requests proceed and which wait, increasing completed tool steps.","reader_setup":[{"name":"Model and data","description":"Qwen3.6-27B served by a fixed vLLM 0.24.0 backend, with 192 distinct SWE-bench Lite coding workflows."},{"name":"Test setup","description":"All 192 workflows run concurrently, each capped at 100 steps. After five minutes of warm-up, formal evaluation counts completed valid steps over 30 minutes; public evaluation uses a ten-minute scored window."},{"name":"Starting point","description":"A working gateway that tracks ongoing agent programs and controls when their requests reach the model server."},{"name":"What the agent changes","description":"Request admission, queue ordering, and scheduling based on observed traffic and the supplied server limits. The model server, coding agents, and tool environments remain fixed."}],"reader_metric":{"meaning":"Measures completed tool steps per minute across all 192 coding agents.","rule":"Score = total completed tool steps ÷ 30 minutes."},"reader_compute":"Worker and Judge run on CPUs; the model backend uses 2 dedicated GPUs. H100 NVL (94 GB) or H100 SXM (80 GB)."}}};
